## ABSTRACT
Hybrid composites made of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and lanthanide-doped upconversion nanoparticles (UCNPs) have attracted considerable interest for their synergistically enhanced functions in various applications such as chemical sensing, photocatalysis, anticounterfeiting and nanomedicine. However, precise assembly of MOF/UCNP hybrid composites with tunable morphologies remains a challenge due to the lack of effective synthetic methods and fundamental understanding of the growth mechanisms. Herein, we propose a modulator-directed assembly strategy to synthesize a series of ZIF-8@UCNP composites (ZIF-8 = zeolitic imidazolate framework-8). The UCNPs densely paved on the surface of ZIF-8 microcrystals and endowed the composites with intense upconversion blue emission, which were verified by steady-state/transient photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy and single-particle imaging. Ethylenediamine (EDA) was firstly used as a modulator to fine-tune the predominant MOF facets and realize distinct morphologies of the composites. By adjusting the concentration of EDA from 0 to 25 mmol/L, the morphology of the ZIF-8@UCNP composites was tuned from rhombic dodecahedron (RD) to truncated rhombic dodecahedron (TRD), cube with truncated edges (CTE), cube, and finally a unique form of interpenetration twins (IT). The nucleation and growth process of the ZIF-8@UCNP composites was monitored by time-dependent scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images and the formation mechanism was thoroughly revealed. Furthermore, we demonstrated that the strategy for assembly of morphology-controllable ZIF-8@UCNP composites was generally applicable to various UCNPs with different sizes and shapes. The proposed strategy is expected to open up new avenues for the controllable synthesis of MOF/UCNP composites toward diverse applications.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, upconversion nanoparticles, hybrid composites, ethylenediamine, modulator
## 1 Introduction
As a well-established class of porous materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are ordered network complexes formed by connecting metal ions/clusters with organic ligands by coordination bonds [1,2]. They have found wide usage in a variety of fields such as gas separation and storage, catalysis, fluorescence sensing, and drug delivery [3–12]. Controllable synthesis of MOFs with different sizes, morphologies, and structures is significant for regulating their behaviors in gas separation, catalysis, medical diagnostics, etc. [13–15]. The main strategy explored so far toward size and morphology control is based on a coordination modulation method, in which different additives, such as polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB), and F127 surfactant, are used to modulate crystal growth by influencing coordination equilibria during the nucleation and growth process [16–18]. Although some progress has been made in recent years, it is highly needed
to further explore effective synthetic methods of MOFs with varying sizes and morphologies and understand the underlying growth mechanisms. Furthermore, the resulting MOFs with tunable sizes and morphologies can be further utilized as matrixes or carriers to construct versatile hybrid composites for diverse applications [19–21].
Lanthanide-doped upconversion nanoparticles (UCNPs) have attracted much attention because of their ability to convert low-energy light (such as near-infrared light) into high-energy light (such as ultraviolet or visible light) [22, 23]. Benefiting from the unique properties of lanthanide ions, UCNPs possess the advantages of adjustable luminescence wavelength, good light stability, low auto-fluorescence background, and low toxicity, thus showcasing a vast variety of promising applications in biosensing, laser, and three-dimensional (3D) display [24–29]. The combination of UCNPs with MOFs can not only provide additional functions to UCNPs, but also greatly broaden their applications in various fields [30–44]. For example, Tang et al.
synthesized core–shell UCNP@Fe-MOF nanoprobes for luminescent/magnetic dual-mode imaging [30]. Li et al. fabricated MOF/UCNP heterodimers for near-infrared (NIR)-induced synergetic therapy of tumors [31]. Jiang et al. designed UCNP-Pt@MOF/Au composites featuring plasmon and upconversion enhanced photocatalysis [32]. Recently, MOF nanosheets were assembled with UCNPs by Yuan et al., showing great advantages in anticounterfeiting [33]. Although MOF/UCNP composites have great application prospects, it still remains challenging to realize controllable combination of the two components due to the lattice mismatch between UCNPs and MOFs and the lack of a deep insight into assembly mechanism of the composites. Recent studies toward MOF/UCNP composites mostly focus on the exploration of new systems and expansion of the scope of their applications. But it is still rare for precise modulation of their morphologies and systematic investigation of the growth kinetic for the composites. Nevertheless, the morphology control and assembly mechanism of the MOF/UCNP composites remain nearly untouched, which hinders the rapid development of these functional materials.
In this study, we develop an effective strategy for precise assembly of ZIF-8@UCNP composites by using ethylenediamine (EDA) as a modulator. The morphology of ZIF-8@UCNP composites can be accurately tuned from rhombic dodecahedron (RD) to truncated rhombic dodecahedron (TRD), cube with truncated edges (CTE), cube, and interpenetration twins (IT). The influence of EDA on the nucleation and growth process of the ZIF-8@UCNP composites are comprehensively surveyed by means of time-dependent scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images, and the formation mechanism was thoroughly revealed. The upconversion performance of the composites was investigated by steady-state/transient photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy and single-particle imaging. Furthermore, we demonstrated that this strategy was applicable to a library of UCNPs, irrespective of size and shape.
