## 2.2 Synthesis of ZIF-8 MCs with five morphologies (ZIF8-RD/TRD/CTE/Cube/IT)
The ZIF-8 MCs with different morphologies were synthesized via a classical solvothermal method with slight modification [45]. \(5~\mathrm{~mL}\) methanol solution of MeIM \((25\;\mathrm{\mmol/L})\) containing different amounts of EDA \((0/5/10/15/25\,\mathrm{\mmol/L})\) , and \(10\,\mathrm{\mL}\) methanol solution of \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) \((11.5\;\mathrm{mmol/L})\) were mixed, and then allowed to react at \(40\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) without stirring. After that, the products were collected by centrifugation at \(12{,}000\ \mathrm{rpm},\) washed several times with \(5\;\mathrm{mL}\) methanol and finally dispersed in \(2\ \mathrm{~mL}\) methanol. The products were named as ZIF-8- RD/TRD/CTE/Cube/IT.
## 2.3 Synthesis of \(\mathbf{NaYF_{4}}\mathbf{:}\mathbf{Yb}/\mathbf{Gd}/\mathbf{Tm}\) nanoparticles (NPs)
The \(\mathrm{NaYF_{4}}\mathrm{:}\mathrm{Yb}/\) Gd/Tm NPs were synthesized via a solid–liquid thermal decomposition method as described previously [46]. For synthesizing 1 mmol of \(191\ \mathrm{nm\NaYF_{4}}{:}20\%\mathrm{Yb}/30\%\mathrm{Gd}/0.5\%\mathrm{Tm}\) UCNPs, 0.495 mmol of \(\mathrm{Y}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{3},\) 0.2 mmol of \(\mathrm{Yb}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{3},\) 0.3 mmol of \(\mathrm{Gd}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{3},\) and \(0.005\ \mathrm{mmol}\) of \(\mathrm{Tm}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{3}\) were mixed with \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) of OA, and \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) of ODE in a \(100\,\mathrm{\mL}\) two-neck round-bottom flask. The resulting mixture was heated to \(180\,^{\circ}\!C\) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) flow with constant stirring for \(30\,\mathrm{\min}\) to form a clear solution, and then cooled down to room temperature (RT). Thereafter, 2 mmol of \(\mathrm{NaHF}_{2}\) and 2 mmol \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COONa}\) powder were added into the solution. The solution was heated to \(250\,^{\circ}\!C\) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) flow with vigorous stirring for \(30\,\mathrm{\min}\) , promoting the decomposition of \(\mathrm{NaHF}_{2}\) and then heated to \(310~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for another \(90\ \mathrm{min}\) . Subsequently, the mixture was allowed to cool down to RT. The obtained NPs were precipitated with \(20~\mathrm{~mL~}\) ethanol, collected by centrifugation, washed with \(3~\mathrm{mL}\) ethanol and \(2~\mathrm{mL}\) cyclohexane several times, and finally re-dispersed into \(5~\mathrm{mL}\) cyclohexane for further usage. We can obtain nanoparticles of different sizes by changing the storage time at \(310~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . The \(\mathrm{NaYF_{4}}\mathrm{:}\mathrm{Yb}/\mathrm{Gd}/\mathrm{Tm}\) NPs with sizes of 115 and \(24~\mathrm{nm}\) can be obtained by the same procedure with that of the \(191~\mathrm{nm}\) ones except for reducing the crystal growth time at \(310\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) from \(90\,\mathrm{{min}}\) to 60 and \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) , respectively.
