## 2.7 Characterization
Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the samples were collected on an X-ray diffractometer (MiniFlex 600; Rigaku, Tokyo, Japan) with Cu Kα1 radiation \((\lambda~=~0.154187~\mathrm{\nm})\) . Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) measurements, including high-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HAADFSTEM) image and energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) element mapping, were performed on an FEI Tecnai F20 TEM (Hillsboro, OR). SEM measurements were performed by using a JSM-6700F SEM. Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectra were measured in a Magna 780 FTIR spectrometer from samples in KBr pellets. \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption and desorption measurements were conducted on a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 instrument and Pore Size Analyzer, which was performed at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\) in a liquid nitrogen bath. Confocal laser scanning microscopy images were obtained with a modified Nikon C2 confocal laser scanning microscope. Dynamic light scattering (DLS) and Zeta potential measurement was conducted on a Zetasizer Nanoseries (Nano ZS90) instrument. Upconversion PL spectra were collected under \(980\,\mathrm{nm}\) laser excitation provided by the corresponding continuouswave laser diode. PL decays were measured with a customized ultraviolet (UV) to mid-infrared steady-state and phosphorescence lifetime spectrometer (FSP920-C; Edinburgh Instruments Ltd., Livingston, UK) equipped with a digital oscilloscope (TDS3052B; Tektronix, Beaverton, OR) and a tunable mid-band optical parametric oscillator (OBO) pulse laser as the excitation source
(410–2,400 nm, \(10\,\mathrm{Hz}\) , and a pulse width of \(\sim5\) ns (Vibrant 355II; OPOTEK, Carlsbad, CA, USA)).
## 3 Results and discussion
Zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) with a chemical formula of \(Z\mathrm{n}_{6}(\mathrm{MeIM})_{12}\) \(\mathrm{(MeIM=2}\) -methylimidazole) is one of the most studied typical ZIF materials. ZIF-8 adopts a sodalite (SOD) zeolite-type structure (space group \(I\bar{4}3m\) ) and has large pores with a diameter of \(11.6\mathrm{~\AA~}\) that are accessible through small apertures with a diameter of \(3.4\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) (Fig. 1(a)(II) [49–52]. A common approach for synthesizing ZIF-8 nano/microcrystals (N/MCs) with various morphologies is the use of surfactants or monodentate ligands as growth inhibitors or capping agents at the reaction system of ZIF-8. For example, monodentate ligands such as 1-methylimidazole, n-butylamine, and sodium formate are the most commonly used crystallization inhibitors for the synthesis of ZIF-8 N/MCs [53, 54]. The role of monodentate ligands can be understood as regulating the formation of deproton balance during the nucleation and growth of ZIF-8 crystals. In 2011, Pan et al. firstly demonstrated the use of surfactants to control the preferential growth of specific crystalline facets of ZIF-8 MCs [18]. They adopted CTAB as a capping agent to isolate pure cubic or TRD ZIF-8 crystals. From then on, CTAB was extensively used to regulate the shape of ZIF-8 N/MCs [55]. In this work, we successfully synthesized a series of ZIF-8 MCs with different morphologies by using EDA as a modulator. EDA can not only increase the alkalinity of the environment for the synthesis of ZIF8 MCs, but also accelerate the deprotonation of MeIM ligands. When \(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\) metal salts reacted with MeIM ligands in a methanol solution at \(40\,^{\circ}\!C\) without EDA, the prepared ZIF-8 MCs adopted a typical rhombic dodecahedron shape (denoted as ZIF-8- RD) with twelve exposed (110) facets and had an average size of
By virtue of the precise morphological modulation and understanding the growth kinetics for ZIF-8 MCs, we further investigated the control synthesis of ZIF-8@UCNP via a stepwise method. Firstly, \(\mathrm{NaYF_{4}{:}Y b/G d/T m}\) UCNPs were synthesized through a facile solid–liquid thermal decomposition method. The TEM image depicted that the as-synthesized UCNPs displayed a good monodispersity and size uniformity with a mean size of \(191\,\mathrm{\nm}\) (Fig. 1(b)(I) and Fig. S3(a) in the ESM). Powder XRD analysis indicated that the resulting UCNPs were pure hexagonal phase with fine crystallization (Fig. S2 in the ESM). Subsequently, the naked UCNPs were derived by washing with hydrochloric acid to remove OA molecules from their crystal surface. Next, the naked UCNPs were coated with PVP. The successful coating of PVP on the surface of UCNPs was confirmed by FTIR spectroscopy (Fig. 1(e)). The FTIR spectrum of OA-UCNPs exhibits the carboxylate (–COO–) stretching frequencies at 1,556 and \(1,441~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) ; while the absorption bands at 2,920 and \(2,850~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are assigned to \(\nu_{\mathrm{as}}(-\mathrm{CH}_{2}-)\) and \(\nu_{\mathrm{s}}(-\mathrm{CH}_{2}-)\) , respectively. After coating the naked-UCNPs with PVP, a strong carbonyl \(\scriptstyle(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O})\) stretching peak at \(1,668~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and a characteristic \(\mathbf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{N}\) stretching
peak at \(1,293~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) were observed, which demonstrated the successful exchange of OA molecules by PVP on the surface of UCNPs. Finally, the PVP-UCNPs were added into the precursor solution of ZIF-8 and subsequently underwent a co-assembly process toward the formation of the ZIF-8@UCNP composites. It is worthy of noting that ZIF-8@UCNP composites with five morphologies can be obtained by changing the content of EDA from 0 to \(25~\mathrm{mmol/L}\) (Figs. 1(b)(II)–1(b)(VI)). Furthermore, the composites exhibited an identical morphological change process to that of the pure ZIF-8 MCs driven by the EDA modulator. In all the prepared ZIF-8@UCNP composites, the \(\mathrm{NaYF_{4}{:}Y b/G d/T m}\) NPs homogeneously paved on the surface of ZIF-8 MCs. Powder XRD analysis and FTIR spectra proved the successful fabrication of the ZIF-8@UCNP composites (Figs. 1(c) and 1(e)). The structures of the ZIF-8@UCNP composites were further confirmed by the HAADF-STEM characterizations (Figs. 2(a)(I) and 2(b)(I) and Figs. S4–S6 in the ESM). The HAADF-STEM image in Fig. 2(a) verifies the successful formation of a core–shell-like structure of ZIF-8-Cube@UCNP composed of ZIF-8 as the core and UCNPs as the shell. Furthermore, energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping images of ZIF-8@UCNP composites indicated that Zn and N elements were distributed uniformly inside, while Na, Y, Yb, Gd, and Tm elements were evenly scattered outside (Fig. 2 and Figs. S4–S6 in the ESM). The compositional distribution of the ZIF-8@UCNP composites was also investigated by means of EDS elemental line scan. High contents of Y and Yb observed at the central section in the EDS line scan profile of ZIF-8- \(\cdot\mathrm{RD}(\varpi)\) UCNP revealed the existence of UCNPs inside the ZIF-8 MCs (Fig. S7(a) in the ESM). By contrast, the profiles of ZIF-8-Cube@UCNP and ZIF-8- \(\mathrm{IT}(\varpi)\) UCNP indicated the distribution of Y and Yb in the peripheral region of the composites, confirming their core–shell-like structure with UCNPs paving on the surface of ZIF-8 MCs (Figs. S7(b) and S7(c) in the ESM). DLS measurements were performed to evaluate the dispersibility of ZIF-8@UCNP composites in aqueous solution (Fig. S8 in the ESM), revealing a low aggregation and uniform distribution of the composites, which was consistent with the SEM results. \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption–desorption analysis revealed that the BET surface area of ZIF-8-CTE@UCNP \((328.1~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) was smaller than that of pure ZIF-8-CTE MCs \((520.2~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) , which should be due mainly to the heavy and nonporous UCNPs (Fig. 1(d) and Table S1 in the ESM).
UCNPs possess excellent NIR-responsive PL properties with
potential applications in diverse fields such as chemical sensing, biological imaging, diagnosis, and therapeutics [56–58]. The as-prepared ZIF-8@UCNP composites exhibited ultraviolet, blue, and red emissions from \( \mathrm{Tm}^{3+} \) ions at 344, 361, 448, 474, and \( 645~\mathrm{nm} \), assigned to the \( \mathrm{I}_{6} \rightarrow \mathrm{F}_{4} \), \( \mathrm{I}_{2} \rightarrow \mathrm{^3H}_{6} \), \( \mathrm{I}_{2} \rightarrow \mathrm{^3F}_{4} \), \( \mathrm{G}_{4} \rightarrow \mathrm{^3H}_{6} \), and \( \mathrm{I}_{4} \rightarrow \mathrm{^3F}_{4} \) transitions, respectively (Fig. 3(a)). The PL intensities of the ZIF-8@UCNP composites were comparable to that of the corresponding UCNPs. The luminescence lifetimes of the \( \mathrm{Tm}^{3+} \) emission at \( 474~\mathrm{nm} \) in the ZIF-8@UCNP composites were in the range of \( 1.0{-}1.2~\mathrm{~ms} \), which are comparable to the pure \( \mathrm{NaYF}_{4} \):Yb/Gd/Tm NPs (Fig. S(b) and Table S2 in the ESM). PL photographs of the ZIF-8@UCNP composites in methanol solution were presented, showing an intense blue emission of \( \mathrm{Tm}^{3+} \) ions (Fig. 3(c)). Furthermore, we conducted confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) measurement for the ZIF-8@UCNP composites (Figs. 3(d) and 3(e)). To obtain the bright-field optical and CLSM images, the ZIF-8@UCNP composites dispersed in methanol were dropped onto a quartz glass slide. The UC luminescence images from a single ZIF-8@UCNP composite were observed upon \( 980~\mathrm{nm} \) laser excitation by means of confocal laser scanning microscopy. The obtained CLSM images further confirmed bright blue emission of the ZIF-8@UCNP composites. We also collected confocal upconversion fluorescence Z-stack acquisition images of ZIF-8-RD@UCNP and ZIF-8-Cube@UCNP
upon laser excitation at \( 980~\mathrm{nm} \). In the Z-stack acquisition images of ZIF-8-RD@UCNP, intense blue light was observed at the entire particle, indicating uniform distribution of the UCNPs in the composite (Fig. S9 in the ESM). In the Z-stack acquisition images of ZIF-8-Cube@UCNP, the top and bottom of the particle emitted light at the entire surface (Figs. S10(b) and S10(h) in the ESM). However, Z-stack acquisition images scanning at the middle of the particle showed the sample emitted from their rims only (Figs. S10(c)–S10(g) in the ESM and Movie ESM1), which further confirmed the core–shell-like structure of ZIF-8-Cube@UCNP. It has proved that adjusting the morphology of MOFs can significantly affect their microstructures and performances in diverse applications, such as gas adsorption, catalysis, drug delivery, antimicrobial and cancer therapy, to name a few [59, 60]. Therefore, morphology control of ZIF-8 crystals is of significance for tuning the functionality of ZIF-8@UCNP composites.
To understand the growth process of ZIF-8 MCs with the modulation of EDA, we monitored the evolution of their morphology by SEM. In the absence of EDA, small spherical ZIF-8 MCs were formed at the early growth stage (Fig. S11 in the ESM). Then, the small ZIF-8 MCs quickly grew up to about \( 500~\mathrm{nm} \) at \( 12~\mathrm{min} \) with some small particles attaching on the crystal surface. With the passage of the reaction time, the small particles disappeared and the ZIF-8 MCs further grew up. When
the reaction time reached \(24\,\mathrm{h}\), ZIF-8-RD MCs with a diameter of \(700\,\mathrm{nm}\) were obtained. This growth process is most likely as a result of Ostwald ripening. In the presence of EDA, the crystal growth process changed. When a small amount of EDA was added, ZIF-8 MCs with irregular morphology were observed within \(1\,\mathrm{min}\), and ZIF-8-TRD MCs consisting of twelve (110) and six (100) crystal facets were obtained with prolonged reaction (Fig. S12 in the ESM). Increasing the content of EDA to \(10\,\mathrm{mmol/L}\), we found that the ZIF-8 cubes with six (100) facets were produced at the early stage (Fig. S13 in the ESM). After \(120\,\mathrm{min}\), (110) crystal facets gradually formed. When the (110) crystal facets further grew, ZIF-8 MCs with a CTE shape were finally prepared. With adding more EDA to \(15\,\mathrm{mmol/L}\), the ZIF-8-Cube formed at the early growing stage was inclined to be stable and matured (Fig. 4(a)). The growth rate of ZIF-8 MCs was significantly reduced by enhancing the content of EDA to \(25\,\mathrm{mmol/L}\). Only irregular ZIF-8 MCs were observed within \(120\,\mathrm{min}\) (Figs. 4(c)(I)–4(c)(III)). The relatively high concentration of EDA provided a stronger alkaline environment than that under other conditions, which may hinder the crystallization of ZIF-8 at the first \(120\,\mathrm{min}\) of the synthetic process. Fantastic ZIF-8-IT MCs were obtained after \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) of the reaction time (Figs. 4(c)(IV)–4(c)(VI)). The above SEM results indicate that we can tune the ratio of (110) and (100) facets and alter the morphology of ZIF-8 MCs from RD to IT by adjusting the concentration of EDA from 0 to \(25\,\mathrm{mmol/L}\). It has been proved that only (100) facets of ZIF-8 possess unsaturated Zn sites [61]. Therefore, we speculate that EDA preferentially coordinated with the unsaturated Zn sites on the (100) facets, thus reduced the surface energy of (100) facets and consequently promoted the formation of ZIF-8 crystals enclosed by (100) facets. In comparison with other modulators for the morphology control of ZIF-8 crystals such as CTAB and F127, EDA can not only act as a capping ligand to preferentially coordinate with the unsaturated Zn sites on the (100) facets of ZIF-8, but also provide an alkaline
environment to impact the nucleation and growth process of ZIF-8 crystals. Therefore, EDA can provide a wider range of morphology control for ZIF-8 crystals than other modulators.
We sought to explore the role of EDA as a modulator in the control growth of ZIF-8@UCNP composites. To this end, we monitored the structural evolution of the composites by SEM in all situations. Without EDA, only a few UCNPs were able to pave on the surface of the irregular ZIF-8 MCs within \(1\,\mathrm{min}\) of reaction time (Fig. S14 in the ESM). With the gradual growth and maturity of ZIF-8 MCs, more UCNPs paved on their surface, and finally formed ZIF-8-RD@UCNP at \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). When \(5\,\mathrm{mmol/L}\) EDA was added, irregular ZIF-8 MCs were formed with very few UCNPs paving on their surface at the early stage before \(12\,\mathrm{min}\) (Fig. S15 in the ESM). The ZIF-8 MCs gradually grew to \(1\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) with regular morphology, and more UCNPs were evenly distributed on the surface of the ZIF-8 MCs within \(120\,\mathrm{min}\). With the extension of the reaction time, the UCNPs gradually covered the surface of ZIF-8 MCs, and finally monodisperse ZIF-8-TRD@UCNP were obtained. The growth processes of the ZIF-8-CTE@UCNP and ZIF-8-Cube@UCNP were similar to that of ZIF-8-TRD@UCNP containing two main stages: the in situ independent growth of ZIF-8 MCs at the first stage and the incorporation of UCNPs and ZIF-8 MCs to form the hybrid composites at the second stage (Fig. 4(b) and Fig. S16 in the ESM). In contrast, when the added amount of EDA was increased to \(25\,\mathrm{mmol/L}\), the nucleation and growth of the ZIF-8 MCs were hindered at the first stage. Only the added UCNPs were observed in the reaction solution within \(120\,\mathrm{min}\). The ZIF-8 MCs cannot be found at this stage, which is consistent with the growth of pure ZIF-8-IT MCs upon addition of \(25\,\mathrm{mmol/L}\) EDA (Figs. 4(d)(I)–4(d)(III)). ZIF-8-IT@UCNP can be finally yielded over time (Figs. 4(d)(IV)–4(d)(VI)).
PVP is widely used as surfactant, surface stabilizer, growth modifier, and dispersant in the construction of various,
nanomaterials [62–64]. The PVP molecule contains hydrophobic long carbon chain, which can prevent the aggregation of the resulting nanomaterials via the steric hindrance effect. Furthermore, PVP is soluble in water and common organic solvents as a result of the highly polar amide and apolar methylene groups within its backbone. Compared with the morphology modulation effect for the composites endowed by EDA, PVP plays an important role in the incorporation of the UCNPs and ZIF-8 MCs. The zeta potential measurements reveal that the PVP-UCNPs, naked-UCNPs, and ZIF-8 MCs all carried positive charges (Fig. S17 in the ESM). Therefore, it is impossible to construct ZIF-8@UCNP composites via electrostatic interactions.
By virtue of the weak coordination interactions between the carbonyl groups within the pyrrolidone rings and zinc ions in ZIF-8 nodes, as well as the hydrophobic interactions between apolar methylene and methine groups of PVP and the organic ligands, the PVP-UCNPs were easily combined with ZIF-8 MCs. Benefiting from the synergistic effect of PVP and EDA, the facile construction of ZIF-8@UCNP composites and precise control over their structures can be achieved. Additionally, the successful synthesis of ZIF-8@UCNP composites based on the UCNPs with different sizes (24, 115, and \(16 \times 75~\mathrm{nm}\)) and shapes (spherical, hexagonal, and rod-like) demonstrated a good universality of this strategy (Fig. 5 and Fig. S3 in the ESM).
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we report the controllable synthesis of ZIF-8@UCNP hybrid composites via an in situ assembly strategy and precisely tune their morphologies by employing EDA as a modulator. Time-dependent SEM analyses indicated that EDA can promote the development of (100) facets and eventually altered the crystal shape of ZIF-8. Therefore, ZIF-8@UCNP composites with five distinct morphologies ranging from RD to TRD, CTE, cube, and IT were successfully obtained. Steady-state/transient PL spectroscopy and single-particle imaging confirmed the intense upconversion blue emission from the composites. Furthermore, we demonstrated that the strategy for assembly of morphology-controllable ZIF-8@UCNP composites was universal and applicable to various UCNPs with different sizes (24, 115, and \(191~\mathrm{nm}\)) and shapes (spherical, hexagonal, and rod-like). We anticipate that the proposed strategy provides a new route to precisely manufacturing luminescent MOF/UCNP composites for versatile applications.
